		<meta property="schema:genre">Poetry</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">A collection of poetry by James Russell Lowell.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/james-russell-lowell"&gt;James Russell Lowell&lt;/a&gt; was a poet, diplomat, and Harvard professor whose poetic work spanned half a century. A literary critic with a sharp wit and deft command of language, his works often took piercing aim at the wider literary world, as in the epic-length poem, “A Fable for Critics.”&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Influenced by his abolitionist wife, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/maria-lowell"&gt;Maria White Lowell&lt;/a&gt;, works such as “Stanzas on Freedom” lambast the moral hypocrisy of slavery. His poem “Anti-Texas” argues against the annexation of Texas on abolitionist grounds, and “The Present Crisis” is a piercing critique of the subsequent Mexican American War.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;His marriage with Maria, though happy, was marked by tragedy; Maria died at the young age of 32, and only one of their four children survived to adulthood. His poems “Trenodia” and “The First Snowfall” are bittersweet laments on the passing of a child.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Listed among the “fireside poets” of the nineteenth century, Lowell believed poets had a duty to speak prophetically in their time. He writes,&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;“While philosophers are wrangling, and politicians playing at snapdragon with, the destinies of millions, the poet, in the silent deeps of his soul, listens to those mysterious pulses which, from one central heart, send life and beauty through the finest veins of the universe, and utters truths to be sneered at, perchance, by contemporaries, but which become religion to posterity.”—&lt;i&gt;Conversations on Some of the Old Poets&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Collected here are all of Lowell’s poetic works, ordered by date of first publication.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
		<dc:language>en-US</dc:language>
